# ================================================================== PART 6 · THE BORROWER NATIONS
flow(id="sovereign", part=5, scope="world",
 title="Government Debt: Interest Before Schools",
 kick="Governments owe 102 trillion dollars, and in dozens of countries the interest now takes more than health or education.",
 sum="Governments borrow by selling bonds, as the mechanics chapter described. Borrowing can build a railway or a power grid that pays for itself. It becomes a trap when the interest grows faster than the government's income. The interest must be paid first, because a government that misses a payment is shut out of borrowing altogether. Everything else in the budget waits.<br><br>Earlier chapters supplied the reasons a poor country's interest runs high: the grade given by three agencies, the pull of American interest rates, and tax that leaves through the havens. This chapter measures the outcome. It gives the world total, counts the people living where interest outranks public services, maps the share of every government's income that goes to interest, and shows the gap in the rates that rich and poor governments are charged.",
 bridge="The first slide gives the size of the world's public debt and where it is growing fastest. The second counts the people who live where interest exceeds spending on health or education. The third maps interest as a share of each government's revenue. The fourth works through what the difference in rates costs.",
 out="Public debt stands at 102 trillion dollars, 3.4 billion people live in countries that pay more in interest than on health or education, and poorer governments are charged two to four times the American rate. The next chapter looks at debts owed in foreign money.",
 rec="In countries that are home to 3.4 billion people, interest on government debt now takes more than health or education.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "UN Trade and Development, A World of Debt 2025", "u": UNDEBT},
      {"t": "UN Trade and Development, news release of 26 June 2025", "u": UNNEWS},
      {"t": "World Bank, Interest payments (per cent of revenue)", "u": WBDATA + "GC.XPN.INTP.RV.ZS"}],
 steps=[
  st("2024", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "One Hundred and Two Trillion Dollars",
   "UN Trade and Development adds up what every government owes. In 2024 the total reached a record 102 trillion dollars, up from 97 trillion the year before. Most of it is owed by rich countries, which borrow cheaply and in their own currencies. Developing countries owe 31 trillion, less than a third. The trouble is in the speed and the price. Since 2010, the report finds, public debt in developing countries has grown twice as fast as in rich ones. And every one of those bonds pays interest to whoever holds it. The holders are banks, pension funds, insurers and the fund managers described in Part 4. So a government's debt is someone else's income. When a finance minister says there is no money for a pay rise or a clinic, part of the reason is that a payment to bondholders is due first. The size of that payment depends on the rate, and the rate was not set by the people who use the clinic.",
   UNNEWS, w=1, pl=["unctad"], ex=["debt2025"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because the figure is a world total. The third slide of this chapter maps the interest burden country by country."),
  st("2024", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "3.4 Billion People Where Interest Comes First",
   "The same report puts the cost in terms of people. In 2024, developing countries paid 921 billion dollars in interest on their public debt. That was 10 per cent more than a year earlier. In a record 61 developing countries, interest took at least a tenth of all government revenue. Then the report makes the comparison that matters most. In 46 developing countries, the government now spends more on interest than on either health or education. About 3.4 billion people live in those countries. That is more than four people in every ten alive. For a child there, it means the state pays more to its lenders than it spends on that child's schooling or on the clinic the family uses. This is not mainly a matter of how much they borrowed. Developing countries owe less than a third of the world's public debt. It is a matter of what they are charged, which the last slide of this chapter sets out. First, the map shows where the burden falls.",
   UNDEBT, w=1, pl=["unctad"], ex=["debt2025"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because the report's summary gives totals for developing countries as a group. The next slide shades every country using World Bank data."),
  st("Latest year", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "How Much of Each Government's Income Goes to Interest",
   "The World Bank reports, for each government, the share of its revenue that is paid out as interest. The map shades the %(intrev_n)s countries with a figure since 2018. In %(intrev_10)s of them, interest takes a tenth of revenue or more. In %(intrev_20)s it takes a fifth or more. Sri Lanka's figure was about 80 per cent in 2023. India, Brazil, Malawi and Zambia are all near or above 30 per cent. The United States, at about 20 per cent, is in the darkest group too, which shows that a rich country can owe a great deal. The difference is what happens next. The United States borrows in the currency it creates, and the Federal Reserve can buy its bonds. A country that owes in dollars it cannot create has no such support. When its interest bill rises, it can only tax more, spend less, or borrow again at a higher rate. Germany's figure is under 3 per cent.",
   WBDATA + "GC.XPN.INTP.RV.ZS", data={"k": "intrev", "only": None}, pl=["worldbank"]),
  st("2020 to 2025", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "The Poorer the Borrower, the Higher the Rate",
   "Why is the bill so heavy where incomes are lowest? UN Trade and Development gives the rates. Since 2020, developing regions have borrowed at two to four times the rate paid by the United States. On ten-year bonds the averages were 2.8 per cent for the United States, 5.5 for Asia and Oceania, 7.1 for Latin America and 9.8 for Africa. Work it through for a loan of one billion dollars to build a hospital system. At 2.8 per cent the interest is 28 million a year. At 9.8 per cent it is 98 million. Over ten years the African government pays 700 million more for the same hospitals, before repaying a cent of the loan itself. Lenders say the higher rate reflects higher risk. But the rate also creates the risk. A government that must find 98 million a year is more likely to fail than one that must find 28 million. The grade, the rate and the risk each push the others up, and the lender is paid for all three.",
   "https://unctad.org/news/developing-countries-face-record-high-public-debt-burdens-now-time-reform", w=1, pl=["unctad", "sp", "moodys", "fitch"], ex=["unctad_rates"],
   note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because the source compares regions. The worked example of a one-billion-dollar loan is arithmetic on the published rates."),
 ])

flow(id="external", part=5, scope="world",
 title="Owing in Someone Else's Money",
 kick="A debt in dollars has to be repaid with dollars, and a country can only get those by selling abroad or borrowing again.",
 sum="There is a difference between a government that owes money in its own currency and one that owes in a foreign one. The first can always find the money, at worst by having its central bank create it. The second cannot. It must earn the foreign currency by exporting, receive it from workers abroad, or borrow it again. This is called external debt, and it is where the chapter on money across borders becomes a matter of daily life.<br><br>Recall the rules from that chapter. If the home currency falls, every dollar owed costs more. If American interest rates rise, so does the interest on floating loans. A country with heavy external debt is therefore exposed to decisions made elsewhere. This chapter maps how much each developing country owes abroad, how much of its export earnings go straight back out as payments, and how often such debts have ended in a crisis.",
 bridge="The first slide maps each country's foreign debt against the size of its economy. The second maps the share of export earnings used to pay it. The third counts the debt and currency crises of the last half-century and returns to the lender described in Part 2.",
 out="In %(extdebt_75)s countries foreign debt exceeds three-quarters of a year's national income, and in %(dserv_20)s a fifth or more of export earnings goes to paying it. Since 1970 there have been 74 government debt crises and 236 currency crises. Part 7 comes down to the household.",
 rec="Countries that owe in foreign money must hand over a large part of what they earn abroad, and are exposed to decisions made elsewhere.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "World Bank, External debt stocks (per cent of GNI)", "u": WBDATA + "DT.DOD.DECT.GN.ZS"},
      {"t": "World Bank, Total debt service (per cent of exports of goods, services and primary income)", "u": WBDATA + "DT.TDS.DECT.EX.ZS"},
      {"t": "L. Laeven and F. Valencia, Systemic Banking Crises Revisited, IMF Working Paper 18/206", "u": "https://www.imf.org/en/publications/wp/issues/2018/09/14/systemic-banking-crises-revisited-46232"}],
 steps=[
  st("Latest year", "Low- and middle-income countries", WORLD, 1.15, "What Each Country Owes Abroad",
   "The World Bank records the external debt of low- and middle-income countries. This is everything owed to lenders outside the country, by the government and by private firms, and nearly all of it is in foreign currency. The Bank compares it with the country's gross national income, which is roughly what the whole country earns in a year. The map shades the %(extdebt_n)s countries with a figure. In %(extdebt_75)s of them, foreign debt is worth three-quarters of a year's income or more. Mozambique, Lebanon, Suriname and Mongolia all owe more than a full year's income, and some of them several times that. Rich countries are left clear, because the World Bank does not collect this figure for them. For the shaded countries, read the tone as exposure. The darker the country, the harder it is hit when its currency falls or when dollar interest rates rise, for the reasons set out in the mechanics chapters. None of those countries has a seat on the committee that sets the dollar rate.",
   WBDATA + "DT.DOD.DECT.GN.ZS", data={"k": "extdebt", "only": None}, pl=["worldbank"]),
  st("Latest year", "Low- and middle-income countries", WORLD, 1.15, "The Share of Export Earnings That Goes Straight Back Out",
   "A foreign debt is paid with what the country earns abroad. The World Bank measures this directly. Debt service is the total paid in a year in interest and repayments. The Bank compares it with the country's earnings from exports and other income from abroad. The map shades %(dserv_n)s countries. In %(dserv_20)s of them, a fifth or more of those earnings is used to pay foreign lenders. Think of what that means on the ground. A country sells its copper, coffee, clothing or oil to the world. Before the money can buy fuel, medicine or machinery, one dollar in five, and in some countries one in two, has already gone back out to creditors. The country then has fewer dollars for imports, its currency weakens, and the remaining debt gets heavier. To keep paying, it borrows again. The exports are real work by real people. A large part of what they earn is spoken for before it arrives.",
   WBDATA + "DT.TDS.DECT.EX.ZS", data={"k": "dserv", "only": None}, pl=["worldbank"]),
  st("1970 to 2017", "Washington", WASH, 2.2, "When It Breaks, the Fund Arrives",
   "Debts like these regularly become impossible to pay. The same researchers who count banking crises for the International Monetary Fund also count the other two kinds. Between 1970 and 2017 they found 74 crises in which a government could not pay its debts and 236 currency crises, in which a currency lost a large part of its value within a year. They found that the three kinds tend to arrive together or one after another. A banking collapse is followed by a currency fall, and then by a government that cannot pay. When that happens, the chain from Part 2 takes over. The government needs dollars. It is not on the list for a swap line. It goes to the Fund, where votes are counted by wealth, and receives a loan in stages on agreed terms. The country keeps its debt, now owed partly to the Fund, and its government has less room to choose than before.",
   "https://www.imf.org/en/publications/wp/issues/2018/09/14/systemic-banking-crises-revisited-46232", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["imf"], ex=["lv2018"],
   note="Shaded: the United States, where the International Monetary Fund has its headquarters. The crisis counts are world totals for 1970 to 2017; the map of banking crises by country is in the third chapter."),
 ])

# ================================================================== PART 7 · THE HOUSEHOLD
flow(id="household", part=6, scope="na",
 title="Household Debt: The Wage That Is Already Spent",
 kick="The chain ends at a household that borrows for a home, a car, an education and the weekly shopping.",
 sum="Every layer above this one earns its living from a payment that somebody at the bottom makes. The bank's interest, the fund manager's fee, the bondholder's income and the shareholder's dividend all begin as part of a wage, a rent, a price or a tax paid by a household. This part of the map arrives at that household.<br><br>The first chapter uses the United States, because its central bank publishes the most detailed count and because its households are among the most indebted on earth. The pattern is the one set out at the very start. Money is created when banks lend, and most of what they lend is to households against their homes. So a family that wants somewhere to live has to take on a share of the country's money supply as a personal debt, and pay interest on it for most of a working life.",
 bridge="The first slide gives the total that American households owe and what it is made of. The second connects that total to the creation of money described in the first chapter. The third shows what the price of money does to a household that borrows on a card.",
 out="American households owe a record 19.1 trillion dollars, 70 per cent of it on their homes. The next chapter turns to people who are charged for using money at all, or shut out of the system.",
 rec="Households carry the debt on which the whole system earns its interest, and most of it is the price of a place to live.",
 roles=["Where the count was made"],
 src=[{"t": "Federal Reserve Bank of New York, Household Debt and Credit Report, third quarter 2026 (10 November 2026)", "u": NYF}],
 steps=[
  st("September 2026", "The United States", [-96, 39], 2.7, "Regional Example: Nineteen Trillion Dollars Owed by Households",
   "Regional Example: The Federal Reserve Bank of New York counts what American households owe every three months. At the end of September 2026 the total was a record 19.1 trillion dollars. It had risen by 5 trillion since the end of 2019. Mortgages, which are loans to buy a home, made up 13.4 trillion. Car loans came to 1.73 trillion and student loans to 1.66 trillion. Credit card balances stood at a record 1.29 trillion. Look at what the list contains. A home, a way to get to work, an education and the gap between pay and prices at the end of the month. These are not luxuries bought on impulse. They are the ordinary requirements of a working life, and in this system each of them comes with a lender attached. Interest on 19 trillion dollars is collected every month, from tens of millions of households, and it flows upward to the banks and investors in the earlier parts of this map.",
   NYF, add={"United States": 0}, pl=["nyfed"], ex=["nyfed2026"], reg=True),
  st("September 2026", "The United States", [-96, 39], 2.7, "Regional Example: Why a Home Comes With a Thirty-Year Debt",
   "Regional Example: Mortgages are 13.4 trillion of the 19.1 trillion, which is 70 per cent. That is no accident. Go back to the first chapter. Banks create money when they lend, and they prefer loans secured on property, because the property can be taken if the borrower stops paying. So most new money enters the economy as mortgages. Now follow the if and the then. If banks lend more freely against houses, buyers can bid more, and prices rise. If prices rise, the next buyer must borrow more. If the next buyer borrows more, more money is created and the bank earns more interest. House prices and household debt climb together, and each step is profitable for the lender. The person who already owns property gains. The person who does not must give a larger share of a lifetime's pay to a bank for the same house. When the central bank cuts its rate to revive the economy, this is one of the main channels it works through.",
   NYF, add={"United States": 0}, pl=["nyfed", "fed"], ex=["nyfed2026", "boe2014"], reg=True),
  st("Today", "The United States", [-96, 39], 2.7, "Regional Example: What Compounding Does on a Card",
   "Regional Example: The smallest item on the list is the most expensive. A credit card charges a far higher rate than a mortgage, because nothing secures the debt. Use the arithmetic from the chapter on interest. Take a balance of 3,000 on a card that charges 20 per cent a year, which is an ordinary rate for a card. The interest is 600 a year, or 50 a month. A household that can spare 50 a month pays the bank 600 a year and still owes 3,000 at the end. It can do that for ten years, pay 6,000, and owe what it owed at the start. People in this position are not careless. They are covering a gap between wages and costs with the most expensive money on offer, because cheaper money is lent to those who already have assets. The 1.29 trillion dollars of card balances is where the price of money is highest and where the borrowers have the least. The lender to that household can itself borrow at the central bank's rate.",
   NYF, add={"United States": 0}, pl=["nyfed"], ex=["nyfed2026"], reg=True,
   note="Shaded: the United States. The card balance and rate in this slide are a worked example in round numbers; the total of card balances is from the source."),
 ])

flow(id="excluded", part=6, scope="world",
 title="The Unbanked, and the Fee on Sending Money Home",
 kick="1.3 billion adults have no account, and migrant workers lose part of every wage they send home to the firms that move it.",
 sum="The last chapter showed a household inside the system and in debt to it. This one looks at people at its edge. In much of the world a person cannot be paid, save or receive money from a relative without paying someone for the service, or cannot do it at all. Money made by banks, as the first chapter showed, lives in bank accounts. A person without an account lives outside it, on cash, and borrows from whoever will lend.<br><br>Then there are the workers who leave home to earn. They clean, build, nurse and harvest in richer countries and send part of their wages back. For whole countries that money is larger than aid and investment. It has to cross the border through a bank or a transfer company, and a slice is kept on the way. This chapter maps who has an account, measures the fee on a wage sent home, and maps the countries that depend on those wages.",
 bridge="The first slide maps the share of adults in each country who have an account. The second gives the cost of sending 200 dollars home and who charges the most. The third maps the countries where money sent by workers abroad is a large part of the whole economy.",
 out="1.3 billion adults have no account, sending 200 dollars home costs about 13 dollars on average and about 30 through a bank, and in %(remit_10)s countries those wages are a tenth of the economy or more. One part remains: why the arrangement stays as it is.",
 rec="The poorest users of money pay the most to move it, and more than a billion adults are outside the system altogether.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "World Bank, Global Findex 2025, press release of 16 July 2025", "u": FINDEX},
      {"t": "World Bank, Account ownership (per cent of population aged 15 and over)", "u": WBDATA + "FX.OWN.TOTL.ZS"},
      {"t": "World Bank, Remittance Prices Worldwide, issue 53 (March 2025)", "u": RPW},
      {"t": "World Bank, Personal remittances, received (per cent of GDP)", "u": WBDATA + "BX.TRF.PWKR.DT.GD.ZS"}],
 steps=[
  st("2024", "Every country in the survey", WORLD, 1.15, "One Adult in Five Has No Account",
   "Every few years the World Bank surveys adults around the world about how they use money. The survey is called the Global Findex. The 2025 edition found that 79 per cent of adults have an account at a bank or with a mobile money service, up from 51 per cent in 2011. That leaves 1.3 billion adults with none. More than half of them, about 650 million, live in eight countries: Bangladesh, China, Egypt, India, Indonesia, Mexico, Nigeria and Pakistan. The map shades %(acct_n)s countries by the share of adults with an account. In %(acct_40)s of them it is under 40 per cent. Consider what the lack of an account means. Wages come in cash and can be stolen. Savings earn nothing. There is no record with which to ask a bank for a loan, so borrowing means a moneylender. The rise in accounts is real, and much of it comes from mobile phones. It also means that hundreds of millions of poor people have recently become paying customers of the system described on this map.",
   FINDEX, data={"k": "acct", "only": None}, pl=["worldbank"], ex=["findex2025"]),
  st("2025", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "Thirteen Dollars to Send Two Hundred",
   "Money that a worker abroad sends home is called a remittance. The World Bank tracks what the transfer costs along hundreds of routes between countries. Its standard test is the cost of sending 200 dollars. In the first quarter of 2025 the world average was 6.49 per cent, which is about 13 dollars. Governments agreed years ago to bring this down to 3 per cent by 2030, and the average has barely moved. The cost depends on who carries the money. Banks are the most expensive channel, at an average of nearly 15 per cent later the same year, or about 30 dollars out of 200. It depends on the destination too. Sending to sub-Saharan Africa cost 8.78 per cent, the highest of any region. Moving money is now a matter of changing two numbers in two computers. The fee does not reflect that cost. It reflects how few firms serve each route and how little choice the sender has. A cleaner sending wages to a village pays a higher rate for the service than a company moving millions.",
   RPW, w=1, pl=["worldbank"], ex=["rpw2025"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because the figures quoted are world and regional averages. The next slide maps where remittances matter most."),
  st("Latest year", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "The Countries That Live on Wages Sent Home",
   "For some countries these transfers are the main source of foreign money. The World Bank compares the remittances each country receives with the size of its economy. The map shades %(remit_n)s countries. In %(remit_10)s of them, remittances equal a tenth of the whole economy or more. In Tajikistan the figure is over half. In Nepal, Honduras and Lebanon it is about a third, and in El Salvador more than a quarter. Put this beside the last two chapters. These are countries with foreign debts to pay and few exports to pay them with. What they export is people. The dollars those people send back pay for imports and keep the currency from falling. A fee of 6 per cent on that flow is a charge on the poorest part of the world's workforce, collected by banks and transfer companies in the richest. And the family at home receives its share only after every layer described on this map has taken its part.",
   WBDATA + "BX.TRF.PWKR.DT.GD.ZS", data={"k": "remit", "only": None}, pl=["worldbank"]),
 ])

# ================================================================== PART 8 · THE SHIELD AND THE WHOLE CHAIN
flow(id="shield", part=7, scope="world",
 title="Why the Rules Stay as They Are",
 kick="An arrangement this costly to most people does not survive by accident. It is defended, and the defence is part of the system.",
 sum="A reader who has come this far may ask why none of this has been changed. Banks create the money, a few committees price it, three firms grade the borrowers, three fund managers hold the votes, and the costs run downhill to households and poor countries. Most of it is legal and much of it is published. It continues because the people who gain from it are close to the people who write the rules, and the people who pay are not in the room.<br><br>That closeness has already appeared in every part of this map. This chapter gathers it in one place, using findings from earlier slides and adding the official verdict on what the supervisors knew before 2008. It is not about a conspiracy. It is about a set of ordinary arrangements: who appoints whom, who funds whom, and who is considered too important to be held to the law.",
 bridge="The first slide sets out what the official inquiry found about the regulators before 2008. The second lists the points at which the industry sits on both sides of the table, each one taken from an earlier chapter.",
 out="The inquiry into 2008 found that the authorities had the power to prevent the crash and did not use it. The industry elects directors of the central bank's branches, is consulted on its own rulebook, pays for its own grades and has been spared prosecution on grounds of size. The last chapter puts the whole chain in one place.",
 rec="The system is kept in place by ordinary arrangements that put the financial industry on both sides of the table.",
 roles=["Where the findings were made"],
 src=[{"t": "Financial Crisis Inquiry Commission, Conclusions (2011)", "u": FCIC},
      {"t": "NPR, Financial Crisis Panel Says Disaster Was Avoidable (27 January 2011)", "u": "https://www.npr.org/sections/itsallpolitics/2011/01/27/133270655/financial-crisis-panel-disaster-was-avoidable"}],
 steps=[
  st("2011", "Washington", WASH, 2.2, "The Referees Could Have Stopped It",
   "The commission that investigated the crash of 2008 for the United States Congress did not blame bad luck. It concluded that the crisis was the result of human action and inaction, and that it could have been avoided. It pointed to widespread failures in financial regulation and supervision, and it named the Federal Reserve for failing to stop the flood of bad home loans, which it had the legal power to do. It found that the people in charge had been warned and had not acted. Several members of the commission disagreed about which causes mattered most, and published their own views. This matters for everything else on the map. The usual defence of placing so much power with unelected experts is that experts will protect the public from the industry. In the most serious test of the last eighty years, the experts had the authority and the information, and the industry was left to carry on until it broke.",
   "https://www.npr.org/sections/itsallpolitics/2011/01/27/133270655/financial-crisis-panel-disaster-was-avoidable", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["fcic", "fed", "sec"], ex=["fcic2011"]),
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "The Same People on Both Sides of the Table",
   "Gather what earlier slides have already shown. Commercial banks hold stock in the regional reserve banks of the United States and elect six of each one's nine directors. The world's bank rulebook is drafted in Basel by central bankers and supervisors, with the banks commenting on the drafts, and reaches parliaments already agreed. The rating agencies are paid by the issuers of the bonds they grade. The fund managers who cast shareholder votes generally vote with the managers of the companies. At the International Monetary Fund, the countries whose lenders are owed the money hold the majority of the votes. And when one of the 29 largest banks admitted to laundering drug money, the prosecutors' own specialists were overruled for fear of the effect on the financial system. Not one of these is a secret. Each is a normal working arrangement, and each places the industry where the decision is made. A system built this way does not need to break rules in order to be protected. The rules are the protection.",
   FCIC, w=1, pl=["fed", "bcbs", "moodys", "blackrock", "imf", "hsbc"], ex=["fed_directors", "bcbs_members", "ratings91", "bigthree88", "imf_votes", "hsbc2012"],
   note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. Each ringed circle is a body from an earlier chapter whose arrangement is recalled here."),
 ])

flow(id="chain", part=7, scope="world",
 title="The Whole Chain",
 kick="From the committee that sets the rate to the household that pays it: the system in one place.",
 sum="Money begins as a loan. A private bank creates it by writing a number into an account, and charges interest on it. The price of that money is set by a central bank committee that voters do not choose and cannot remove. The amount of risk a bank may take is fixed in Basel by a committee of central bankers, and it allows a bank to operate with 3 of its own for every 100 at risk. Twenty-nine banks are officially too large to fail, which lets them borrow cheaply and bet heavily. When the bets failed in 2008, the system was rescued, and households lost homes, jobs and savings.<br><br>More than half of the world's financial assets now sit with funds outside the bank rules. Three fund managers are the largest shareholder in most big American companies. A tenth of households own nearly all the shares, and companies return record sums to them. A third of multinationals' foreign profit is booked in havens, and about half a trillion dollars of tax goes missing each year.<br><br>The cost runs downhill. Three rating firms grade every government. The poorest pay several times the rate of the richest, and in countries with 3.4 billion people the interest takes more than health or education. Countries that owe in dollars must follow an interest rate set for the United States, and when they fail they borrow from a Fund where votes are counted by wealth. At the bottom is a household: in debt for its home, charged the highest rate on its smallest loan, paying a fee to send its wages to its family, or holding no account at all.<br><br>Each link is legal, and most are published by the institutions themselves. Taken one at a time they look like technical details. Taken together they describe one arrangement. The power to create money and to set its price is held by private firms and by bodies beyond the reach of a vote. The gains go to those who already own assets. The costs go to those who have only their work to sell.",
 bridge="", out="", rec="", roles=[], src=[], steps=[])
